1
00:00:00,335 --> 00:00:01,885
When you buy a share of Apple

2
00:00:02,022 --> 00:00:03,647
Apple doesn't get a single cent of

3
00:00:03,647 --> 00:00:04,535
your money.

4
00:00:05,252 --> 00:00:06,302
So who does?

5
00:00:06,302 --> 00:00:07,627
And if the company doesn't get the

6
00:00:07,627 --> 00:00:08,227
money,

7
00:00:08,152 --> 00:00:10,327
why does the stock market even exist

8
00:00:11,135 --> 00:00:11,785
To answer that,

9
00:00:11,785 --> 00:00:13,235
we only need one thing.

10
00:00:13,235 --> 00:00:14,710
A lemonade stand.

11
00:00:20,018 --> 00:00:20,843
Meet Penny.

12
00:00:20,843 --> 00:00:22,405
Penny sells lemonade.

13
00:00:22,405 --> 00:00:23,755
A cup sells for a dollar,

14
00:00:23,755 --> 00:00:25,818
and costs about forty cents to make

15
00:00:26,568 --> 00:00:27,443
On a good summer,

16
00:00:27,443 --> 00:00:29,243
she sells a thousand cups.

17
00:00:29,243 --> 00:00:31,393
That's six hundred dollars of profit.

18
00:00:32,035 --> 00:00:33,560
Penny wants a second stand,

19
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:34,635
across town.

20
00:00:34,635 --> 00:00:36,360
That needs a thousand dollars she doesn't

21
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:37,110
have.

22
00:00:37,727 --> 00:00:38,852
She could borrow it.

23
00:00:38,852 --> 00:00:40,202
But a loan has to be paid

24
00:00:40,202 --> 00:00:40,802
back,

25
00:00:40,514 --> 00:00:41,327
with interest,

26
00:00:41,327 --> 00:00:43,177
even if it rains all summer.

27
00:00:43,793 --> 00:00:45,618
So she has another idea.

28
00:00:47,893 --> 00:00:49,718
She splits her business into one hundred

29
00:00:49,718 --> 00:00:50,955
equal slices.

30
00:00:50,955 --> 00:00:52,993
These slices are called shares.

31
00:00:53,627 --> 00:00:55,052
Each share is one percent of the

32
00:00:55,052 --> 00:00:55,865
stand.

33
00:00:55,865 --> 00:00:57,090
One percent of the cups,

34
00:00:57,090 --> 00:00:57,690
the profits,

35
00:00:57,689 --> 00:00:58,752
and the future.

36
00:00:59,393 --> 00:01:00,743
She keeps sixty shares,

37
00:01:00,743 --> 00:01:02,493
and sells forty to her neighbours,

38
00:01:02,493 --> 00:01:04,343
for twenty-five dollars each.

39
00:01:04,935 --> 00:01:06,897
Penny gets her thousand dollars.

40
00:01:06,897 --> 00:01:08,660
She builds the second stand.

41
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:10,222
And her neighbours now own part of

42
00:01:10,222 --> 00:01:11,260
her business.

43
00:01:11,993 --> 00:01:13,743
Grandma bought ten shares.

44
00:01:13,743 --> 00:01:14,731
With two stands,

45
00:01:14,731 --> 00:01:16,793
the business now makes twelve hundred dollars

46
00:01:16,793 --> 00:01:17,631
a year.

47
00:01:17,631 --> 00:01:19,993
Ten percent of that belongs to Grandma

48
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:21,560
Some of the profit,

49
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:23,010
Penny keeps to grow.

50
00:01:23,010 --> 00:01:23,610
Some,

51
00:01:23,410 --> 00:01:25,322
she pays out to her shareholders.

52
00:01:25,322 --> 00:01:27,435
That payout is called a dividend.

53
00:01:29,727 --> 00:01:31,527
This is exactly what Apple did in

54
00:01:31,527 --> 00:01:32,677
nineteen eighty.

55
00:01:32,677 --> 00:01:34,339
It sold shares to the public for

56
00:01:34,339 --> 00:01:35,464
the first time.

57
00:01:35,464 --> 00:01:37,302
That's called an I. P. O.,

58
00:01:37,302 --> 00:01:39,127
an initial public offering.

59
00:01:39,785 --> 00:01:42,460
Apple sold shares at twenty-two dollars each

60
00:01:42,585 --> 00:01:45,010
and raised about a hundred million dollars

61
00:01:45,793 --> 00:01:47,493
That first sale is when the company

62
00:01:47,493 --> 00:01:48,518
gets the money.

63
00:01:48,518 --> 00:01:49,943
And it's almost the only time it

64
00:01:49,943 --> 00:01:50,743
does.

65
00:01:53,035 --> 00:01:53,660
Because now,

66
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:55,472
Grandma wants a new bicycle.

67
00:01:55,472 --> 00:01:56,985
She doesn't want to own lemonade any

68
00:01:56,985 --> 00:01:57,585
more.

69
00:01:57,572 --> 00:01:58,785
She wants cash.

70
00:01:59,427 --> 00:02:00,977
She can't make Penny buy her shares

71
00:02:00,977 --> 00:02:01,577
back.

72
00:02:01,577 --> 00:02:02,777
But she can sell them to someone

73
00:02:02,777 --> 00:02:03,602
else.

74
00:02:03,602 --> 00:02:04,202
Sam,

75
00:02:04,002 --> 00:02:04,677
next door,

76
00:02:04,677 --> 00:02:06,727
offers thirty dollars a share.

77
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:09,485
Grandma gets three hundred dollars.

78
00:02:09,485 --> 00:02:10,985
Sam gets ten shares.

79
00:02:10,985 --> 00:02:11,685
And Penny?

80
00:02:11,685 --> 00:02:12,910
Penny gets nothing.

81
00:02:12,910 --> 00:02:14,035
The money went from Sam,

82
00:02:14,035 --> 00:02:15,135
to Grandma.

83
00:02:15,927 --> 00:02:17,389
That's the stock market.

84
00:02:17,389 --> 00:02:19,277
Not a place where companies get money

85
00:02:19,352 --> 00:02:20,965
but a place where owners trade with

86
00:02:20,965 --> 00:02:21,877
each other.

87
00:02:22,468 --> 00:02:24,393
When you buy Apple shares today,

88
00:02:24,393 --> 00:02:26,318
you're buying them from another investor who

89
00:02:26,318 --> 00:02:27,406
wanted to sell.

90
00:02:27,406 --> 00:02:28,993
Apple isn't involved.

91
00:02:29,652 --> 00:02:30,252
So,

92
00:02:29,839 --> 00:02:31,402
the answer to our question.

93
00:02:31,402 --> 00:02:33,077
Your money goes to whoever sold you

94
00:02:33,077 --> 00:02:34,002
the share.

95
00:02:36,327 --> 00:02:37,677
So why does the price matter to

96
00:02:37,677 --> 00:02:38,727
Penny at all?

97
00:02:39,335 --> 00:02:41,098
Because the price is the town's vote

98
00:02:41,098 --> 00:02:42,872
on what her business is worth.

99
00:02:42,872 --> 00:02:45,335
A hundred shares at thirty dollars means

100
00:02:45,335 --> 00:02:47,685
the whole stand is worth three thousand

101
00:02:48,460 --> 00:02:50,560
That's called market capitalisation.

102
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:52,447
And a high price helps Penny.

103
00:02:52,447 --> 00:02:53,935
Next time she needs money,

104
00:02:53,935 --> 00:02:55,735
she can sell new shares for more

105
00:02:56,685 --> 00:02:58,210
So what moves the price?

106
00:02:58,210 --> 00:02:59,410
Just two things.

107
00:02:59,410 --> 00:03:00,672
How many people want to buy,

108
00:03:00,672 --> 00:03:02,235
and how many want to sell.

109
00:03:02,927 --> 00:03:04,402
A heatwave is forecast.

110
00:03:04,402 --> 00:03:06,415
Everyone expects record sales.

111
00:03:06,415 --> 00:03:07,765
Buyers rush in.

112
00:03:07,765 --> 00:03:09,802
Thirty dollars becomes fifty.

113
00:03:10,427 --> 00:03:11,889
Then a rumour says a big chain

114
00:03:11,889 --> 00:03:13,214
is opening a juice bar on the

115
00:03:13,214 --> 00:03:14,177
same street.

116
00:03:14,177 --> 00:03:15,515
Sellers rush out.

117
00:03:15,515 --> 00:03:17,202
Fifty becomes twenty.

118
00:03:17,877 --> 00:03:19,790
Nothing about the lemons changed.

119
00:03:19,790 --> 00:03:22,077
Only what people expect about the future

120
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:25,910
Then it gets strange.

121
00:03:26,618 --> 00:03:29,118
Word spreads that lemonade shares only ever

122
00:03:29,118 --> 00:03:29,956
go up.

123
00:03:29,956 --> 00:03:31,243
People start buying,

124
00:03:31,243 --> 00:03:32,543
not for the lemonade,

125
00:03:32,543 --> 00:03:34,243
but because they expect to sell to

126
00:03:34,243 --> 00:03:35,618
someone else for more.

127
00:03:36,235 --> 00:03:37,960
The price hits a thousand dollars a

128
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:38,772
share.

129
00:03:38,772 --> 00:03:40,747
A business making twelve hundred dollars a

130
00:03:40,747 --> 00:03:41,347
year,

131
00:03:41,310 --> 00:03:43,260
priced at a hundred thousand.

132
00:03:43,868 --> 00:03:45,281
It would take more than eighty years

133
00:03:45,281 --> 00:03:47,080
of profits to earn that back.

134
00:03:47,080 --> 00:03:48,218
That's a bubble.

135
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:52,048
Not everyone is fooled.

136
00:03:52,048 --> 00:03:53,810
A trader called Rex thinks the price

137
00:03:53,810 --> 00:03:54,935
is crazy.

138
00:03:55,535 --> 00:03:57,410
He borrows ten shares from Sam,

139
00:03:57,410 --> 00:03:59,235
and promises to give ten shares back

140
00:03:59,235 --> 00:04:00,047
later.

141
00:04:00,047 --> 00:04:01,247
Then he sells them,

142
00:04:01,247 --> 00:04:01,985
right away,

143
00:04:01,985 --> 00:04:03,760
for a thousand dollars each.

144
00:04:04,368 --> 00:04:05,443
If the price crashes,

145
00:04:05,443 --> 00:04:07,243
he can buy ten shares back cheap

146
00:04:07,343 --> 00:04:08,356
return them to Sam,

147
00:04:08,356 --> 00:04:09,655
and keep the difference.

148
00:04:09,655 --> 00:04:11,018
That's short selling.

149
00:04:11,635 --> 00:04:12,747
It's a bet that the price will

150
00:04:12,747 --> 00:04:13,410
fall.

151
00:04:13,410 --> 00:04:14,735
And it's dangerous.

152
00:04:14,735 --> 00:04:16,097
If the price keeps rising,

153
00:04:16,097 --> 00:04:17,885
his losses have no limit.

154
00:04:20,227 --> 00:04:21,314
Then autumn arrives.

155
00:04:21,314 --> 00:04:22,102
Sales slow.

156
00:04:22,102 --> 00:04:22,877
Somebody sells.

157
00:04:22,877 --> 00:04:24,377
Then everybody sells.

158
00:04:24,993 --> 00:04:25,993
A thousand dollars.

159
00:04:25,993 --> 00:04:26,693
Five hundred.

160
00:04:26,693 --> 00:04:27,293
A hundred.

161
00:04:27,206 --> 00:04:28,043
Forty.

162
00:04:28,785 --> 00:04:30,785
Rex buys back ten shares at forty

163
00:04:30,785 --> 00:04:31,560
dollars,

164
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:33,135
hands them back to Sam,

165
00:04:33,135 --> 00:04:35,510
and keeps nine thousand six hundred dollars

166
00:04:36,302 --> 00:04:37,489
The people who bought at the top

167
00:04:37,489 --> 00:04:39,152
have lost almost everything,

168
00:04:39,152 --> 00:04:40,202
on paper.

169
00:04:40,927 --> 00:04:42,302
And Penny's lemonade?

170
00:04:42,302 --> 00:04:44,165
It tastes exactly the same.

171
00:04:44,165 --> 00:04:44,965
Two stands.

172
00:04:44,965 --> 00:04:46,627
Twelve hundred dollars a year.

173
00:04:46,627 --> 00:04:48,189
The business never changed.

174
00:04:48,189 --> 00:04:49,677
Only the price did.

175
00:04:50,560 --> 00:04:52,222
That's the whole stock market.

176
00:04:52,222 --> 00:04:54,410
A company sells slices of itself once

177
00:04:54,485 --> 00:04:55,773
to raise money.

178
00:04:55,773 --> 00:04:56,373
After that,

179
00:04:56,348 --> 00:04:58,098
the owners trade the slices with each

180
00:04:58,098 --> 00:04:58,785
other.

181
00:04:59,402 --> 00:05:01,077
The price is just the latest deal

182
00:05:01,077 --> 00:05:02,852
between a buyer and a seller.

183
00:05:02,852 --> 00:05:04,302
It's what people expect,

184
00:05:04,302 --> 00:05:05,752
not what the business is.

185
00:05:06,527 --> 00:05:07,590
So when you buy a share of

186
00:05:07,590 --> 00:05:08,202
Apple,

187
00:05:08,202 --> 00:05:09,902
you're not giving Apple money.

188
00:05:10,593 --> 00:05:12,318
You're buying a slice of its future

189
00:05:12,418 --> 00:05:14,443
from someone who'd rather have the cash

